# Thrive — environment variables.
#
# This file is COMMITTED and contains placeholders only. Never put a real value
# in it. The live values live in .env.local (gitignored) and in Vercel's
# project settings.
#
# Only the push variables are documented here so far, because they are the ones
# that were added without anywhere explaining where they come from. Six months
# from now that explanation is the difference between five minutes and an
# afternoon.

# ── Push notifications ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_BASE64
#
#   WHERE: Firebase console → thrive-career → Project settings (gear icon) →
#          Service accounts tab → "Generate new private key" → Generate key.
#          A JSON file downloads. Keep it out of the repo entirely.
#
#   WHY BASE64 RATHER THAN THE JSON ITSELF. The file is multi-line and its
#   private_key field contains literal \n escapes. Pasted into a dotenv file as
#   raw or single-quoted JSON, those escapes survive as backslash-n and the key
#   silently fails to parse — the classic FCM "Invalid PEM formatted message".
#   Base64 has no escaping, no quoting and no newlines: it is one opaque line
#   that survives copy-paste, this file, and Vercel's env box identically.
#   Decoded once at startup and JSON.parsed.
#
#   TO PRODUCE IT (PowerShell, one line):
#     [Convert]::ToBase64String([IO.File]::ReadAllBytes("C:\path\to\service-account.json"))
#   or in Git Bash:
#     base64 -w0 /c/path/to/service-account.json
#
#   Paste the result with NO quotes and no line breaks.
FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_BASE64=

# FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID
#
#   WHERE: Firebase console → Project settings → General → Project ID.
#          Currently: thrive-career
#
#   It is also inside the service-account JSON. It is kept separately as a
#   GUARD: the dispatcher compares the two and refuses to start if they differ,
#   so a service account for the wrong project fails loudly at boot instead of
#   sending into a project nobody is watching.
FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=

# PUSH_DISPATCH_SECRET
#
#   NOT from Firebase. A shared secret proving that a call to the dispatcher
#   route came from our own database trigger and not from the internet.
#   The same value goes in three places: here, Vercel, and Supabase Vault
#   (where the trigger reads it from — it is never hard-coded in the SQL).
#
#   TO PRODUCE ONE:
#     openssl rand -hex 32
PUSH_DISPATCH_SECRET=
